{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"33db822871c9789418788d97d1f091bf4e8279c70a7f01386077ffe63570c1ad","slug":"markdown.preface","location":"/markdown/preface.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Preface","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Javier Sabio González","given":"Javier Sabio","family":"González"},"name":"Javier Sabio González","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"github":"https://xaviweise.github.io/aaat/","numbering":{"title":{"offset":1}},"source_url":"https://xaviweise.github.io/aaat//blob/main/markdown/preface.md","edit_url":"https://xaviweise.github.io/aaat//edit/main/markdown/preface.md","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"preface.md","url":"/build/preface-6c4a90fd6c4f8498a6354ad9e9ea1976.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Algorithmic trading sits at the intersection of several mathematical disciplines — probability theory, stochastic calculus, statistical inference, optimisation, and machine learning — applied to one of the most complex and dynamic systems human societies have built: financial markets. Each of these disciplines has an extensive literature of its own, and so does the practical craft of building trading systems. What is harder to find is a treatment that integrates them — that derives the Avellaneda–Stoikov market-making equations from first principles in the same breath as it explains how a Kalman filter tracks a fair mid-price, or that connects Bayesian decision theory to the design of a client quoting strategy.","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"oc0n1M6rPE"}],"key":"SLqS3eOJK1"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This book is my attempt at that integration. It grew out of lecture notes developed across several teaching contexts: internal courses for my team at BBVA, where the focus was always on problems we faced in practice; a graduate course on algorithmic trading at the Universidad de Alcalá; and courses at the Instituto de Empresa. Each audience shaped the material differently — practitioners pushed for concrete models and implementable intuitions, while students pushed for accessible introductions to the core subjects — and the tension between those two demands is, I hope, visible in a positive way throughout these pages. The starting point for all of it was my own work at BBVA on algorithmic trading problems, from optimal execution in equity markets to market making in fixed income and FX. The result is a treatment that tries to be mathematically rigorous without sacrificing the practical intuition that makes models useful.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"YsK5CJSrWH"}],"key":"rC7lJlsBhW"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The book is, and will remain for some time, a work in progress. I release it in regular updates and welcome feedback from readers through the issue tracker on GitHub.","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"YQfJwm93n6"}],"key":"wV74BEKZiZ"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prior Knowledge","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"TwkITaU4j1"}],"label":"prior-knowledge","identifier":"prior-knowledge","html_id":"prior-knowledge","key":"KIdUbmAdt7"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The mathematical prerequisites are those of a strong undergraduate degree in mathematics, physics, engineering, or a quantitatively oriented economics or finance programme. Specifically, I assume familiarity with:","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"pCTgkSuBgP"}],"key":"dslsyzKVmv"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calculus and linear algebra","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"OhlFuIOejS"}],"key":"srOtp2xq69"},{"type":"text","value":": partial derivatives, the chain rule, integration, matrix operations, eigendecompositions, and the singular value decomposition. These are used throughout without review.","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"m8DkfWbpjF"}],"key":"kP2XigyvTS"}],"key":"GZuVlCQisR"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Probability and statistics","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"KpQzFmojj9"}],"key":"XfdK4A7fTj"},{"type":"text","value":": probability spaces, random variables, expectations, conditional distributions, the Gaussian family, basic hypothesis testing, and the method of maximum likelihood. The ","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"iCdwsYMJQl"},{"type":"link","kind":"ref","identifier":"intro_bayesian","label":"intro_bayesian","url":"/markdown/intro-bayesian","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/markdown.intro-bayesian.json","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bayesian Modelling","key":"oKoU1BCi34"}],"key":"tI14ykY79w"},{"type":"text","value":" chapter introduces the Bayesian perspective from first principles, but a frequentist background is assumed.","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"WDYaKmQ61T"}],"key":"W51cDgp0LM"}],"key":"akawlqEjGb"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Python","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"BkI8jMVMoP"}],"key":"SUvDcwFWIb"},{"type":"text","value":": the numerical examples and all figures are produced in Jupyter notebooks using standard scientific computing libraries (NumPy, SciPy, Matplotlib, pandas, scikit-learn). Readers comfortable with Python at an intermediate level should be able to run and modify the notebooks without difficulty.","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"aNA0NWW9K9"}],"key":"P9mztfuQW7"}],"key":"LMzjTToSDs"}],"key":"Scgn8CeRmx"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prior knowledge of finance or economics is helpful but not strictly required. The ","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"EDxQQfxOkQ"},{"type":"link","kind":"ref","identifier":"intro_financial_markets","label":"intro_financial_markets","url":"/markdown/intro-financial-markets","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/markdown.intro-financial-markets.json","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Financial Markets","key":"sGFBbGiPoI"}],"key":"XMxROAACOd"},{"type":"text","value":" and ","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"mbzl7vZ9p3"},{"type":"link","kind":"ref","identifier":"intro_financial_instruments"