}],"key":"o2HwLg7LDF"},{"type":"text","value":", ","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"key":"upXwB0XI9F"},{"type":"link","kind":"ref","identifier":"optimal_investment_theory","label":"optimal_investment_theory","url":"/markdown/optimal-investment-theory","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/markdown.optimal-investment-theory.json","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optimal Investment Theory","key":"SRgS7ZZ5gx"}],"key":"Qn83BLUiED"},{"type":"text","value":") covers systematic investment strategies: the construction and evaluation of signals, the principles of backtesting and avoiding overfitting, and the mathematical foundations of the main strategy families — mean reversion, momentum, statistical arbitrage, factor models, and portfolio optimisation including Markowitz mean-variance optimisation, risk parity, and the Black–Litterman framework.","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"key":"YswoAI5ziX"}],"key":"Rd7xgvztH5"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The natural reading order is Parts I through V sequentially. Readers primarily interested in execution can proceed from Part I directly to Part III, returning to Part II as individual chapters require it. Similarly, readers focused on market making will find that ","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"key":"Qd0GU6aKNK"},{"type":"link","kind":"ref","identifier":"intro_bayesian","label":"intro_bayesian","url":"/markdown/intro-bayesian","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/markdown.intro-bayesian.json","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bayesian Modelling","key":"HPOt4aOmzU"}],"key":"YUppUg3FEI"},{"type":"text","value":", ","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"key":"y9NhZzIUDr"},{"type":"link","kind":"ref","identifier":"stochastic_calculus","label":"stochastic_calculus","url":"/markdown/stochastic